{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>איש</b>. Some interpret it as possessions, like רכוש, and the word חסר and the word מלא follow one path, both being intransitive verbs even though they require a construct linkage. The sense is: when one says 'so-and-so is חסר,' one must add to specify what he lacks — whether wisdom, wealth, or greatness. Behold: חסר משוגעים אני — and it is construct, its meaning being 'lacking all desire of his soul.' It is in the heavy binyanim a transitive verb, like ותחסרהו מעט מאלהים; שמו חסר מעט מאלהים. And the one who said it governs two objects erred, for it is found with the added heavy [form], and the diminisher — its meaning is not 'he produced a lack'; rather, it is like a transitive verb, like העדיף."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The Hebrew repeatedly says כמו (like) where an English reader expects a direct identification; translated literally throughout. The phrase הענין כשיאמר האומר is rendered hyper-literally; the construction is elliptical. The disputed grammatical terminology (בנינים הכבדים 'heavy binyanim,' פועל יוצא 'transitive verb') is preserved as technical terms without paraphrase."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:27:55.224686829Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}